slos.description:
compare the following classification systems of living organisms:
a. two-kingdom and five-kingdom classification system of Whittaker,
b. five-kingdom classification by Whittaker and five- kingdom classification system by Lynn Marguilis and Karlene Schwartz;

differentiate between the following types of nutrition in bacteria:
a. autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition,
b. symbiotic and parasitic nutrition;

describe the different modes of reproduction in bacteria:
a. asexual reproduction (binary fission, budding and spore formation),
b. genetic recombination (conjugation, transduction and transformation);

explain the role of harmful bacteria in:
a. human and animal health,
b. food spoilage;

describe specific structural adaptations that enable fungi to thrive in diverse environments such as in:
a. arid deserts: (e.g., thick cell walls, spore formation, fast-spreading hyphae, lack of flagellated cells, thick-walled zygote),
b. extreme temperatures: (e.g., production of cryoprotective compounds such as glycerol and trehalose to protect from freezing damage, heat-shock proteins, thermophilic enzymes and specialised pigment melanin);

differentiate between:
a. acraniata and craniata,
b. urochordata and cephalochordata;

define the terms:
a. photolysis,
b. photophosphorylation;

explain mechanisms involved in opening and closing of stomata:
a. starch sugar hypothesis,
b. influx of potassium ions;

differentiate between:
a. antigen and antibodies,
b. self-antigens and non-self-antigens;

differentiate between:
a. spring/ summer and autumn/winter wood,
b. heartwood and sapwood;

classify movements exhibited by plant as:
a. movement of locomotion,
b. movement of curvature;

describe the types of movement of locomotion with examples:
a. autonomic (ciliary/ flagellar movements, amoeboid movements and cyclosis),
b. paratonic (phototactic, chemotactic and thermotactic);

explain types and sub-types of movement of curvature:
a. autonomic movement [i.e., growth movements (nutation, circumnutation, epinastic and hyponastic movements)],
b. paratonic or induced movement [i.e., (tropic and nastic movements)] with examples;

describe the functions of:
a. bone cells (osteogenic/ stem cells, osteoblasts, osteocytes and osteoclasts),
b. cartilage cells (chondroblasts and chondrocytes);

differentiate between:
a. growth and development,
b. embryo and larva;

differentiate between:
a. amitosis and mitosis,
b. karyokinesis and cytokinesis;

analyse the applications of:
a. polymerase chain reaction,
b. DNA fingerprinting;
